  "summary": "Offensive-security CLI toolkit with pentesting payloads and local API server",
  "details": "Package ships a penetration-testing / red-team toolkit CLI. Files under lib/nexus/, lib/toolkit/, and the top-level darknode.js / darknode-legacy.js contain large data tables of offensive commands (nmap, nikto, sqlmap, hydra, john, hashcat, masscan, nuclei, wpscan, curl/wget probes, ping sweeps, nslookup) and corresponding engine code that spawns those tools via child_process when the operator explicitly invokes a CLI subcommand. lib/nexus/api-server.js stands up a local HTTP server that exposes endpoints shelling out via exec(\"/bin/sh...\") and spawn(\"/bin/bash...\"); lib/nexus/git-security.js carries a SENSITIVE_EXTENSIONS list (.pem/.key/.env/.credentials/etc.) used for scanning target repos; lib/nexus/incident-response.js references no-ip and duckdns as example dynamic-DNS indicators in reference material. Buffer.from(..., \"base64\") usage in lib/toolkit/encoders.js and lib/toolkit/jwt.js is standard JWT/base64 decoding, not payload de-obfuscation. No lifecycle scripts fetch or execute remote code at install time, no top-level module code reads installer secrets (~/.aws, ~/.ssh, ~/.npmrc, browser stores, env dumps) and ships them off-host, no hardcoded author-controlled C2 is bound to a POST, and no self-propagation or backdoor mechanism targets the host that installs the package. Offensive capability is directed at operator-chosen external targets at explicit CLI invocation time.",
